{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwGkEDEaGAHwzbVy1e1i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S44BGjcNnaYmXq4TpvkChc","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\nq=[\n(\"grokking generalization beyond overfitting small algorithmic datasets modular arithmetic\",\"core_method\",\"Anchors the delayed-generalization phenomenon and the modular-arithmetic task family used in every run.\"),\n(\"progress measures for grokking mechanistic interpretability modular addition transformer\",\"benchmark_context\",\"Defines the small one-block transformer / modular-addition setting and Fourier-circuit view that the width sweeps build on.\"),\n(\"grokking circuit efficiency memorization versus generalization circuits\",\"baseline_comparator\",\"Circuit-efficiency explanation is the main competing account to the memorization-capacity crossover hypothesis (idea 1).\"),\n(\"grokking transition from lazy to rich training dynamics\",\"baseline_comparator\",\"Lazy-to-rich account is the comparator behind the width-aware output-scaling variant (idea 2).\"),\n(\"Omnigrok loss landscape weight norm grokking\",\"baseline_comparator\",\"Weight-norm / loss-landscape explanation and initialization-scale dependence relevant to init-scale and weight-decay choices.\"),\n(\"unifying grokking and double descent model size\",\"baseline_comparator\",\"Connects grokking to double descent in model size and data fraction; relevant to the width-by-train-fraction sweep.\"),\n(\"maximal update parametrization width-independent feature learning hyperparameter transfer\",\"architectural_shift\",\"Idea 2 introduced muP-like width-aware output scaling and idea 3 a W=m*V hidden-layer reparametrization; muP is the literature behind both.\"),\n(\"effective learning rate width scaling normalization weight decay AdamW\",\"result_driven_theme\",\"Idea 3 tests an effective-learning-rate confound in width; decoupled weight decay (AdamW) and effective-LR dynamics are needed to interpret it.\"),\n(\"memorization capacity of models bits per parameter\",\"result_driven_theme\",\"Memorization-capacity crossover hypothesis (t_mem falls with width, critical width scales with train fraction) needs capacity-estimation and memorization-dynamics work.\"),\n(\"deep double descent model size epochs test error\",\"baseline_comparator\",\"Width-wise non-monotonicity comparator for any claim that delay vs width is monotone, flat, or non-monotone (final log-slope CI spans zero).\"),\n]\n# keep 9: drop Omnigrok? keep; drop deep double descent merge with unifying\nq=[x for x in q if not x[0].startswith(\"deep double\")]\nplan={\n\"status\":\"ready\",\n\"central_topic\":\"How network width (model size) relates to the onset delay of grokking on modular-addition tasks in small transformers, tested via a memorization-capacity crossover hypothesis with a width-by-train-fraction sweep, right-censored delay metrics, bootstrap slope CIs, and muP-style parametrization controls.\",\n\"core_methods\":[\"grokking delay measurement with right-censoring on modular addition\",\"width x train-fraction sweeps of small transformers with bootstrap CIs on log-delay slope\",\"memorization-capacity crossover hypothesis (t_mem vs t_gen, critical width vs train fraction)\",\"width-aware output scaling (muP-like) and hidden-layer W=m*V reparametrization to control effective learning rate\",\"weight decay / AdamW training\"],\n\"comparators\":[\"circuit-efficiency explanation of grokking\",\"lazy-to-rich training dynamics\",\"weight-norm / loss-landscape (Omnigrok) account\",\"grokking and double descent unification\",\"memorization-capacity and memorization-dynamics literature\"],\n\"benchmarks\":[\"modular addition mod prime p, exhaustive p^2 input pairs (Power et al. algorithmic datasets)\",\"one-block transformer on modular addition (Nanda et al.)\"],\n\"architectural_shifts\":[\"standard parametrization to width-aware output scaling (OUT_EXP, UNEMBED_INIT)\",\"hidden-layer reparametrization W=m*V on qkv/proj/fc1/fc2 (HIDDEN_EXP)\",\"last-position-only transformer forward\"],\n\"result_driven_themes\":[\"final sealed evaluation: fitted log-delay vs width slope -0.020 with bootstrap CI [-0.58, 0.35] spanning zero, vs reference slope -0.37; 40% of runs censored (no grokking within budget) -> conditional/inconclusive width effect\",\"held-out controls (shuffled labels) stay near chance (max val acc ~0.012), so detected generalization is genuine\",\"effective-learning-rate confound between width and delay under reparametrization\",\"censored/survival-style delay estimation\"],\n\"queries\":[{\"query\":a,\"category\":b,\"rationale\":c} for a,b,c in q],\n\"forbidden_query_patterns\":[\"generic 'optimization', 'benchmarking', 'scientific method', 'paper writing' phrasing\",\"chronology/search-history queries (cycle order, idea numbering, combined score)\",\"queries phrased as 'effect of model size/width on grokking delay' or 'scaling of grokking with model size' (risk of surfacing the excluded prior paper; the starter literature plus component literatures suffice)\",\"internal harness terms: eligible, rubric, worker, program_1\",\"LLM scaling-law or large-language-model pretraining queries not tied to memorization capacity\"],\n\"failure_reason\":\"\"}\njson.dump(plan,open('reference_query_plan.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nmd=\"\"\"# Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n## Central topic\n%s\n\n## Grounded evidence driving the plan\n- Three model variants were evaluated: a memorization-capacity crossover sweep (width x train fraction), a width-aware output-scaling (muP-like) variant, and a hidden-layer W=m*V reparametrization targeting an effective-learning-rate confound.\n- Sealed final evaluation: log-delay-vs-width slope -0.020, bootstrap CI [-0.58, 0.35] (spans zero), reference slope -0.37; censored fraction 0.4; shuffled-label control max val acc ~0.012. The width effect is therefore conditional/inconclusive rather than a clean monotone law.\n- Setting: small transformer, modular addition mod prime p, exhaustive p^2 pairs, weight decay (AdamW).\n\n## Queries\n\"\"\" % plan[\"central_topic\"]\nfor i,x in enumerate(plan[\"queries\"],1):\n    md+=f\"{i}. `{x['query']}` ({x['category']}) — {x['rationale']}\\n\"\nmd+=\"\"\"\n## Comparators expected by reviewers\nCircuit efficiency, lazy-to-rich dynamics, weight-norm/Omnigrok, double descent, memorization capacity.\n\n## Forbidden / avoided patterns\n\"\"\"+\"\\n\".join(\"- \"+x for x in plan[\"forbidden_query_patterns\"])+\"\"\"\n\n## Why these over generic alternatives\nEach query maps to a specific hypothesis or control actually run (capacity crossover, muP-style scaling, effective-LR confound, censored delay under weight decay). Generic queries (\"model scaling\", \"optimization\", \"benchmarking\") were rejected as low-precision. Direct \"model size vs grokking delay\" queries were deliberately avoided to respect the task's exclusion of the prior paper; the width question is covered through component literatures instead. No retrieval was performed in this stage.\n\"\"\"\nopen('reference_query_plan.md','w').write(md)\nnote=\"\"\"\n## Reference query plan (planning stage)\n- Wrote reference_query_plan.{md,json}: %d queries covering grokking origin, mechanistic modular-addition transformer, circuit efficiency, lazy-to-rich, Omnigrok, grokking/double descent, muP, effective LR/AdamW, memorization capacity.\n- Rejected generic queries (optimization, benchmarking, scaling laws of LLMs) and direct 'model size grokking delay' queries (risk of excluded prior paper). Final grounded result: width slope CI spans zero, 40%% censored.\n\"\"\" % len(q)\nfor f in ['memory.md','global_memory/memory.md']:\n    open(f,'a').write(note)\nopen('global_memory/memory.jsonl','a').write(json.dumps({\"step\":\"reference_query_plan\",\"scope\":\"planning\",\"summary\":\"Wrote 9-query plan grounded in capacity-crossover, muP-style scaling, effective-LR confound, censored delay; rejected generic and excluded-paper-shaped queries.\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-11T14:10:00+00:00\"})+\"\\n\")\nprint(len(q))\nEOF"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1944,"cache_read_input_tokens":32600,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1944},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c27022ae-c5ad-4fe0-9e86-378bc3c28091","uuid":"0170172a-4319-457e-9652-8ed97b6c1ab6","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:08:49.353Z","request_id":"req_011CgwGkDtdyGd1Sr8dvAyQ7"}
